Absolutely. Here's a tip for game designers.

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:52.560
I like to call how to deal with a tight deadline.

00:35:56.640 --> 00:35:59.860
Complete a full game as quickly as possible.

00:36:02.180 --> 00:36:07.140
And then go back and enhance until they pry the code from your hands.

00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:09.820
After that, stop.

00:36:10.200 --> 00:36:11.280
Capital letters.

00:36:11.880 --> 00:36:14.360
Adding features and only fix bugs.

00:36:14.580 --> 00:36:17.620
Or you'll simply fall victim to creeping elegance.

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:20.900
Give your game a beginning, middle, end, and end.

00:36:20.900 --> 00:36:29.820
If the code release deadline comes and all you have is a fully playable and beautifully tweaked main game, all of your work is commercially worthless.

00:36:30.200 --> 00:36:31.880
That game can't be released.

00:36:32.420 --> 00:36:40.800
Implement the complete game flow from the time to player boots up until the final congratulations screen, which is funny if you think about it.

00:36:41.680 --> 00:36:47.460
Do this first, even if the fun part, the main gameplay sequence, is nothing but a placeholder.

00:36:47.940 --> 00:36:54.960
Second, make the game really fun, but leave out neat little enhancements that might take a day or two to the code.

00:36:55.480 --> 00:37:05.220
Then, in the last week of the project, revisit each part of the sequence based on greatest need and add those cool little ideas that have been percolating in your brain.

00:37:05.220 --> 00:37:14.580
The result is that near the end of the allocated time, you have an average but complete game that has at least some commercial value.

00:37:15.600 --> 00:37:27.000
But since everybody knows that a great game sells better than an average game, you now have the leverage to show your publisher that you can improve the game with each subsequent day they can give you.

00:37:27.200 --> 00:37:29.600
So, this guy thinks he's got it figured out.

00:37:29.920 --> 00:37:35.840
