00:52:43.680 --> 00:52:52.800
Games have borrowed so much from film and television in the way that they present themselves nowadays that there really isn't a need for there to be a television show when...

00:52:52.800 --> 00:53:05.320
If you're playing Last of Us, like, these cut scenes are kind of Hollywood quality in their cinematography and in their voice acting and all that fun stuff.

00:53:05.320 --> 00:53:08.580
So, there's less of a...

00:53:08.580 --> 00:53:12.020
There's less of an itch that I want to see that as a movie.

00:53:12.160 --> 00:53:13.340
It's already a movie.

00:53:13.860 --> 00:53:21.180
Absolutely. And then having it as a movie, you're taking away a lot of time and you're taking away a layer of engagement that you have.

00:53:21.420 --> 00:53:30.000
Yeah. Like, I think you get more connected to characters in video games because you're playing as them and because you're literally, like, living and dying with them.

00:53:30.500 --> 00:53:35.780
Yeah. And then it's kind of just a matter of, like, what does a movie add to it?

00:53:35.840 --> 00:53:37.700
And usually, the movie subtracts from it.

00:53:37.860 --> 00:53:40.180
Like, literally, it's subtracting a layer of engagement.

00:53:40.340 --> 00:53:41.840
Like, you're not playing it.

00:53:42.480 --> 00:53:44.800
Yeah. The only movie...

00:53:44.800 --> 00:53:46.360
The only video game movie...

00:53:46.360 --> 00:53:48.380
It's very hard to tell the difference.

00:53:48.660 --> 00:53:57.300
The only movie that I've seen that is based on a video game that I have actually really enjoyed for all the wrong reasons was Silent Hill.

00:53:57.480 --> 00:54:00.900
And that's only because it was fan service for most of it.

00:54:01.040 --> 00:54:02.840
It's like, oh, that's the actual music.

00:54:03.000 --> 00:54:06.000
And, like, oh, those are the actual, like, enemy models.

00:54:06.200 --> 00:54:08.380
Like, it wasn't because it was a good movie at all.

00:54:08.960 --> 00:54:11.340
So, yeah, you're not watching them for the same reasons.

00:54:11.840 --> 00:54:13.920
Yeah, I don't know what it is, but there's...

00:54:13.920 --> 00:54:21.460
I actually have no appetite to see Sonic the Hedgehog, Super Mario Brothers, Super Smash Brothers, Legend of Zelda.

00:54:21.460 --> 00:54:24.200
I don't have any appetite to watch those things on film.

00:54:24.380 --> 00:54:26.640
I want the next Legend of Zelda game.

00:54:26.640 --> 00:54:29.180
I don't want to see the next Legend of Zelda movie.

00:54:29.460 --> 00:54:37.420
Yeah, I would trade every piece of Silent Hill media that is outside of video games just for one more real game.

00:54:38.100 --> 00:54:39.640
That was beautiful, Sean.

00:54:40.320 --> 00:54:48.400
Real fast, let me walk you through what we're not going to play, since we are going to play Ghostbusters 2, if you remember way back, like, an hour ago when we were talking about sequels and spinoffs.

00:54:48.900 --> 00:54:51.620
We're also not going to play the real Ghostbusters.

00:54:51.680 --> 00:55:03.660
I think it's weird that they made that the title of the game, the real Ghostbusters, implying that they know that you just franchised out this company in the NES game, and now you get to play as, like, Bill Murray for real this time.

00:55:03.660 --> 00:55:05.400
Oh, is this the cartoon?

00:55:06.700 --> 00:55:13.960
You know, I don't know what this is, but it was released in the arcades in 1987, and I don't know if the cartoon got out that fast.

00:55:14.240 --> 00:55:30.640
I'm looking at the Wikipedia page for the real Ghostbusters, and it seems that the primary piece of media called the real Ghostbusters was an animated television show that ran from September of 86 to October of 91.

00:55:31.400 --> 00:55:40.940
Yeah, I mean, I'm seeing that too now, so that's pretty crazy that they got the animated series out that fast, but that just goes to show you that, like, they knew Ghostbusters was going to be a hit.

00:55:41.140 --> 00:55:42.480
All of these things were planned.

00:55:42.820 --> 00:55:43.960
Yeah, it was all planned.

00:55:44.820 --> 00:55:46.420
So that's what the real Ghostbusters is.

00:55:46.540 --> 00:55:48.460
Sorry, Ghostbusters fans listening to this episode.

00:55:48.460 --> 00:55:52.660
I don't know anything about the Ghostbusters, other than that the first movie is pretty good.

00:55:54.220 --> 00:55:57.060
There's this whole line of extreme Ghostbusters, too.

00:55:57.220 --> 00:55:58.760
Sean, do you want to see if that's animated?

00:55:58.760 --> 00:56:00.440
It is a...

00:56:00.440 --> 00:56:01.560
It's like eight extreme Ghostbusters games.

00:56:01.760 --> 00:56:03.000
I'm looking at it right now.

00:56:03.200 --> 00:56:04.500
It was in 97.

00:56:04.500 --> 00:56:06.240
I think that's all that lasted for.

00:56:06.260 --> 00:56:09.540
Okay, there you go. So there's a whole line of extreme Ghostbusters games.

00:56:09.740 --> 00:56:20.380
Then finally, in 2009, a Ghostbusters game based on, you know, like with the Bill Murray and Dan Aykroyd characters from the original movie,

00:56:20.380 --> 00:56:24.360
they come in as a full video game, and apparently it's very, very good.

00:56:24.380 --> 00:56:27.200
I never played it, but I heard lots of good things about it.

00:56:27.200 --> 00:56:39.300
But it's not taking many cues from this NES game, but it does have on like a TV somewhere, the congratulation screen is like a scene if you can look for it.

00:56:39.740 --> 00:56:40.540
That's good.

00:56:40.680 --> 00:56:46.460
But so you're saying that there's no, like you don't have to balance the books at Ghostbusters HQ then?

00:56:46.740 --> 00:56:48.620
Not in this game? Right. That's my understanding.

00:56:48.620 --> 00:56:55.260
I mean, I'm sure there's money involved, but it's not like the idea is running a business in Ghostbusters 2009.

00:56:56.060 --> 00:57:01.740
Yeah. It's, yeah, it's much more of like an action adventure game with levels, I believe.

00:57:02.140 --> 00:57:10.060
Okay. Yeah. And then there was a really bad Ghostbusters game in 2016 just called Ghostbusters.

00:57:10.260 --> 00:57:14.060
So that's confusing because in 2009 there was a good one that came out.

00:57:14.160 --> 00:57:16.540
So in 2016 there was a bad one that came out.

00:57:16.620 --> 00:57:18.120
So what would you do in 2019?

00:57:18.460 --> 00:57:21.480
Well, why don't you just re-release the other one called Ghostbusters?

00:57:21.700 --> 00:57:26.380
Surely nobody will be confused, especially if you refer to it as like remastered.

00:57:26.380 --> 00:57:29.020
They won't think you're remastering the game from just three years ago.

00:57:29.200 --> 00:57:35.900
They'll know that you're remastering the 2009 Ghostbusters game and not the 1988 NES Ghostbusters game.

00:57:38.560 --> 00:57:41.000
So that's where I stand on all this stuff.

00:57:41.320 --> 00:57:54.340
I'm kind of glad that we don't have to play too many of these Ghostbusters games, but, you know, I'm sure for Ghostbusters fans, any additions to the lore must have been fun and well.

00:57:54.900 --> 00:58:03.580
For sure. And one thing that's for sure, Sean, is that we are for sure going to do our votes for the Essential Games list.

00:58:06.320 --> 00:58:07.460
Okay, real quick.

00:58:07.480 --> 00:58:17.600
I just think it's funny that I'm on the Wikipedia page for the Ghostbusters video game and it shows it's like the awards that it was nominated for.

00:58:18.000 --> 00:58:25.900
And so for the Spike Video Game Awards, Bill Murray was nominated but did not win Best Performance by a Human Male.

00:58:28.780 --> 00:58:29.840
Oh my God.

00:58:30.380 --> 00:58:32.100
What else is there?

00:58:32.220 --> 00:58:34.240
Best Performance by an AI Robot?

00:58:34.520 --> 00:58:35.720
I don't know. I don't know.

00:58:35.820 --> 00:58:36.760
It's just so weird.

00:58:38.260 --> 00:58:40.760
That's so funny by a human male.

00:58:42.000 --> 00:58:43.380
You're going to buy this game later.

00:58:43.380 --> 00:58:46.060
Not today, but I'll think about it.

00:58:46.220 --> 00:58:50.580
Okay, we are going to go in order of Ghostbusters knowledge for these.

00:58:50.580 --> 00:58:53.220
So, Joe, you will go first.

00:58:53.440 --> 00:58:57.960
Give us your vote for the Essential Games list on Ghostbusters for the NES.

00:58:58.660 --> 00:59:00.200
Not going to be a big surprise.

00:59:00.800 --> 00:59:02.660
I don't think this is essential.

00:59:02.940 --> 00:59:08.640
I mentioned that I thought the gameplay with the streams could be good.

00:59:08.640 --> 00:59:14.060
I think that I should clarify that that is only with extensive, extensive tweaking.

00:59:14.240 --> 00:59:15.620
I think it's the only thing that had the most potential.

00:59:15.860 --> 00:59:20.240
But otherwise, I don't really think anything about this game was particularly fun.

00:59:20.440 --> 00:59:24.680
Going to pass. Okay, and then following in second place, that would be me.

00:59:24.860 --> 00:59:27.100
I clearly don't know that much about the Ghostbusters.

00:59:27.100 --> 00:59:37.000
So, I will say that, you know, this game is a very bizarre, somehow interesting approach to a Ghostbusters video game.

00:59:37.080 --> 00:59:42.380
It's definitely not even on, like, the top five ways I would have imagined making a Ghostbusters game.

00:59:42.540 --> 00:59:51.700
But I can't help feel that an action platformer version where you start at the New York Public Library as, like, level one.

00:59:51.920 --> 00:59:55.260
And then there's, like, a boss ghost at the end of each level.

00:59:55.260 --> 00:59:57.460
Kind of like Castlevania, you know?

00:59:57.620 --> 01:00:02.720
And you work your way all the way up to the fight at the top against the Stay Puft Marshmallow Man.

01:00:02.860 --> 01:00:06.040
I think that would have just been more appreciated by everyone.

01:00:06.220 --> 01:00:07.880
People who actually bought the game.

01:00:08.080 --> 01:00:09.960
People who were fans of the series.

01:00:10.300 --> 01:00:11.920
People who were fans of video games.

01:00:11.980 --> 01:00:15.680
Like, I feel like that would have connected a lot better than what we actually got here.

01:00:15.740 --> 01:00:17.260
So, this is not essential for me.

01:00:17.640 --> 01:00:23.000
Sean? Yeah, this game is 100% not essential.

01:00:23.000 --> 01:00:31.920
But in terms of, like, the experiences that I have to go through for any given episode of this podcast,

01:00:31.920 --> 01:00:38.880
I would say that I at least enjoyed talking about this much more than a lot of the games that we've talked about recently.

01:00:38.880 --> 01:00:47.000
Whether they be just games based on game shows or just shmups.

01:00:47.000 --> 01:00:53.420
At least this was a new concept being explored.

01:00:53.420 --> 01:00:56.820
And then they dropped the flashlight and fell to their death in the cave.

01:00:57.040 --> 01:01:01.840
But this was entertaining to at least talk about.

01:01:02.020 --> 01:01:04.040
So, for that, I give it big ups.

01:01:04.220 --> 01:01:05.020
But still unessential.

01:01:05.580 --> 01:01:07.480
Okay. And I think that's what you have.

01:01:07.660 --> 01:01:13.040
Again, you know, one thing I will say is we're all good sports about bad NES games.

01:01:13.040 --> 01:01:16.200
You know, we can find the silver lining somewhere.

01:01:16.900 --> 01:01:28.920
And I feel like the only games that I've ever played on this system that I couldn't understand why they were ever made was Stack Up and Urban Champion.

01:01:29.520 --> 01:01:34.580
But only Urban Champion because that's, like, a game Nintendo still likes to this day.

01:01:34.740 --> 01:01:35.740
And it's like, really?

01:01:35.940 --> 01:01:37.400
I forgot about Urban Champion.

01:01:37.400 --> 01:01:38.660
To this day? Okay.

01:01:38.660 --> 01:01:40.460
So, those are the two that confused me.

